      "text": "Given that \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is a right triangle with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we know that \\( AB \\) is the length of the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\), and \\( AC \\) and \\( BC \\) are the lengths of the sides adjacent to \\(\\angle A\\). We are given \\( AB = 16 \\) and \\( BC = 24 \\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the length of the hypotenuse \\( AC \\). We can use the Pythagorean theorem, which states:\n\\[\nAC^2 + AB^2 = BC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\nAC^2 + 16^2 = 24^2\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 + 256 = 576\n\\]\nTo isolate \\( AC^2 \\), we subtract 256 from both sides:\n\\[\nAC^2 = 576 - 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 320\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{320} = \\sqrt{64 \\cdot 5} = 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\sin A\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\sin\\) of an angle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. For \\(\\angle A\\), the opposite side is \\( AB \\) and the hypotenuse is \\( BC \\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\nSimplifying the fraction:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).",
